---
Part III, Chapter 2: The classifier stage
Package: packages/loom-lang/src/ast/WeftClassifier.ts
Language: TypeScript
---

# The classifier stage

`WeftClassifier` labels each line with its weft kind by mode. It is a Mealy machine: the state is the previous weft and whether a heading has been seen, the input is a pattern probe of the line, and the output weft also feeds the next state. The wefts it emits are partial — the leading marker filled, the weft `incomplete` — and the tokeniser settles the rest.

## The classifier service

The classifier reads a loom's lines in order, and carries a state from each line to the next. `classifyWefts` takes the range of every line and gives one weft for each. Effect's `Array.mapAccum` does the carrying. It hands `lineStep` the state and one range, and `lineStep` gives back the next state beside the line's weft. At the end `Array.mapAccum` gives the last state beside the wefts, and `classifyWefts` keeps the wefts. A loom's text is whole in memory before the classifier starts, so the pass is a plain fold over an array.

The state opens before the first heading, in the document preamble. It keeps the weft of the line before, whether a heading has been emitted, and whether the line sits inside a frontmatter block. A `---` toggles the frontmatter flag: the first one opens the block, and the next closes it.

A fence in prose opens a quote. Inside a quote a person shows a loom verbatim, so a `#`, `=>`, or `~` there reads as text, not as a mark. Only a fence that matches the opening one closes the quote: one at the same column, at least as long, with nothing after it. So the state keeps the open quote's `Fence`, not a flag, and a shorter run of backticks inside the quote stays text.

A box is a third such region. A line of three colons opens it, and a line of three colons alone closes it. The body between the two stays inert, so a box never reads as loom structure. A box and a quote never open inside each other. The state keeps whether a box is open, because the same line of three colons alone opens a box outside one and closes the box inside one.

The state also keeps whether the loom is a discussion, which its identity line says, since a discussion reads its lines by rules of its own.

Fenced code keeps two more pieces of state. An arrow with nothing after it on its line leaves the classifier waiting for a fence, and blank lines keep it waiting. If the first line that is not blank opens a fence, that line opens fenced code, and the state holds its `Fence` until the line that closes it. While fenced code is open, every line is code that no heading can close, so a Markdown file or a commented script keeps its `#` lines. Any other line ends the wait, and the block is plain code.

=>

type ClassifierState = {
  readonly prev: Option.Option<LoomWeft>
  readonly seenHeading: boolean
  readonly inFrontmatter: boolean
  readonly quote: Option.Option<Fence>
  readonly inBox: boolean
  readonly discussion: boolean
  readonly fenced: Option.Option<Fence>
  readonly awaitingFence: boolean
}

const initialState: ClassifierState = {
  prev: Option.none(),
  seenHeading: false,
  inFrontmatter: false,
  quote: Option.none(),
  inBox: false,
  discussion: false,
  fenced: Option.none(),
  awaitingFence: false,
}

const lineStep =
  (text: string) =>
  (state: ClassifierState, range: LineRange): readonly [ClassifierState, LoomWeft] => {
    const lineText = text.slice(range[0], range[1])
    const weft = boxed(probeWeft(lineText, range, state), state)
    const frontmatterFence = weft.type === 'FrontmatterWeft' && weft.fence !== undefined
    return [
      {
        prev: Option.some(weft),
        seenHeading: state.seenHeading || weft.type === 'HeadingWeft',
        inFrontmatter: state.inFrontmatter !== frontmatterFence,
        quote: nextQuote(state.quote, state.inBox, weft),
        inBox: nextBox(state.inBox, weft),
        discussion: state.discussion || (state.inFrontmatter && namesDiscussion(lineText)),
        fenced: nextFenced(state.fenced, weft),
        awaitingFence: nextAwaiting(weft, state.awaitingFence),
      },
      weft,
    ]
  }

export class WeftClassifier extends Context.Service<WeftClassifier>()('WeftClassifier', {
  make: Effect.succeed({
    classifyWefts:
      (text: string) =>
      (ranges: ReadonlyArray<LineRange>): ReadonlyArray<LoomWeft> =>
        Array.mapAccum(ranges, initialState, lineStep(text))[1],
  }),
}) {
  static readonly layer = Layer.effect(this, this.make)
}

## Classifying one line

`probeWeft` is the decision table. A `---` on the first line opens a frontmatter block, and every line through the closing `---` is a frontmatter weft; outside that block the rest of the table takes over. A fence in prose opens a quote the same way. Every line inside it is verbatim until the fence closes, so a `#`, `=>`, or `~` there stays text — the quote shows a loom, it does not compose one. A box is inert in just the same way, so its body never composes. A quoted line, and a line within a box, keeps the run it stands in, as `keepsPreamble` tells from the line before it. Such a line is a preamble weft before the first heading, where nothing else may appear, and within a section's opening prose. Within the prose after a tilde, it is a prose weft. So a note in a section's opening prose leaves the opening prose whole, and the section's code still begins at its first arrow. A `#` line is a heading in any mode, and opens a section in preamble mode. Before the first heading every line is a preamble weft, since no transition fires there. After it, a line of three or more dashes is a divider in any mode, since a divider ends code as well as prose, just as a heading does. A discussion then reads its lines by rules of its own, which `discussionWeftOf` keeps, before any mode decides. After it, the previous weft's mode decides. In preamble, `=>` opens code and `~` opens prose. In code, `~` returns to prose and every other line stays code. In prose, `=>` re-enters code and every other line stays prose. A line of dashes elsewhere is ordinary content, classified by the current mode like any other line — there is no separator weft.

Fenced code outranks every rule after the quote and the box. Inside it, the line that closes the fence is a fence weft, and every other line is code: a `#` line, an arrow, a tilde, a line of dashes. A waiting arrow comes next, and a fence there opens fenced code. Both checks run before the heading rule, since a heading is exactly what fenced code exists to hold. A quote or a box still comes first, so fenced code quoted in prose stays prose.

=>

const probeWeft = (lineText: string, range: LineRange, state: ClassifierState): LoomWeft => {
  const line = Option.match(state.prev, {
    onNone: () => 1,
    onSome: (w) => w.position.end.line + 1,
  })

  if (Option.isNone(state.prev) && isFrontmatterFence(lineText))
    return makeFrontmatterFence(lineText, line, range)

  if (state.inFrontmatter) {
    if (isFrontmatterFence(lineText)) return makeFrontmatterFence(lineText, line, range)
    return makeFrontmatterField(lineText, line, range)
  }

  if (Option.isSome(state.quote) || state.inBox)
    return state.seenHeading && !keepsPreamble(state.prev)
      ? makeProseWeft(lineText, line, range)
      : makePreambleWeft(lineText, line, range)

  if (Option.isSome(state.fenced))
    return closesFence(state.fenced.value)(lineText)
      ? makeFenceWeft(lineText, line, range, 'close')
      : makeCodeWeft(lineText, line, range)

  if (state.awaitingFence && Option.isSome(openingFence(lineText)))
    return makeFenceWeft(lineText, line, range, 'open')

  const probe = probeOf(lineText)

  if (probe.type === 'heading') return makeHeadingWeft(lineText, line, range, probe.m)

  if (!state.seenHeading) return makePreambleWeft(lineText, line, range)

  if (isDivider(lineText)) return makeDividerWeft(lineText, line, range)

  if (state.discussion) return discussionWeftOf(probe, lineText, line, range, state.prev)

  return readerIn(modeOf(state.prev))(probe, lineText, line, range)
}

~

The last rows of the table belong to the three modes. `preambleLine`, `codeLine`, and `proseLine` each read a line by its probe, and `readerIn` gives the one a mode calls for. The classifier reads every line of every loom, so each reader is a table of cases keyed by the probe's tag, built once when the module loads. A probe is tagged by `type`, as a node is, so `byTypeOr` from the node foundation builds the table. A reader names the probes it treats in a way of their own, and every other probe takes the reader's default. Each case receives the probe and then the line's text, its number, and its range, which `Line` names together. The cases of one reader give wefts of different kinds, so each table states that it gives a `LoomWeft`. `readers` holds the three by mode.

=>

type Line = [lineText: string, line: number, range: LineRange]

type LineReader = (probe: Probe, ...line: Line) => LoomWeft

const preambleLine: LineReader = byTypeOr<Probe, Line>()<LoomWeft>(
  {
    arrow: (arrow, lineText, line, range) => makeArrowWeft(lineText, line, range, arrow.m),
    tilde: (tilde, lineText, line, range) => makeTildeWeft(lineText, line, range, tilde.m),
  },
  (_probe, lineText, line, range) => makePreambleWeft(lineText, line, range),
)

const codeLine: LineReader = byTypeOr<Probe, Line>()<LoomWeft>(
  { tilde: (tilde, lineText, line, range) => makeTildeWeft(lineText, line, range, tilde.m) },
  (_probe, lineText, line, range) => makeCodeWeft(lineText, line, range),
)

const proseLine: LineReader = byTypeOr<Probe, Line>()<LoomWeft>(
  { arrow: (arrow, lineText, line, range) => makeArrowWeft(lineText, line, range, arrow.m) },
  (_probe, lineText, line, range) => makeProseWeft(lineText, line, range),
)

const readers: Readonly<Record<Mode, LineReader>> = {
  preamble: preambleLine,
  code: codeLine,
  prose: proseLine,
}

const readerIn = (mode: Mode): LineReader => readers[mode]

## The lines of a discussion

A discussion holds no code, as the chapter "Discussions" describes, so two marks change their meaning in one. A tilde opens a message wherever it stands, since there is no code for it to close, and an arrow opens nothing, so its line reads as prose. `discussionWeftOf` reads a line of a discussion by those rules: a tilde line is a tilde weft, whose prose is the name of the message's author, and every other line is prose, or preamble before the first message. `namesDiscussion` tells the frontmatter line that names a discussion, `Discussion 1: Waking` alone or joined to its part.

=>

const discussionLine = /^\s*(Part\s+[^,:]+,\s*)?Discussion(\s+[^:]*)?:/

const namesDiscussion = (lineText: string): boolean => discussionLine.test(lineText)

const discussionWeftOf = byTypeOr<
  Probe,
  [...line: Line, prev: Option.Option<LoomWeft>]
>()<LoomWeft>(
  { tilde: (tilde, lineText, line, range) => makeTildeWeft(lineText, line, range, tilde.m) },
  (_probe, lineText, line, range, prev) =>
    modeOf(prev) === 'preamble'
      ? makePreambleWeft(lineText, line, range)
      : makeProseWeft(lineText, line, range),
)

## Line positions

`linePos` is a whole-line span; `span` is an arbitrary offset range on a line. Both stamp the line number the classifier tracks.

=>

const linePos = (line: number, range: LineRange): Position => ({
  start: { line, offset: range[0] },
  end: { line, offset: range[1] },
})

const span = (line: number, start: number, end: number): Position => ({
  start: { line, offset: start },
  end: { line, offset: end },
})

## Mode and probe

Two axes drive the table. `modeOf` derives the mode — preamble, code, or prose — from the previous weft's type, which `modeAfter` reads; a frontmatter weft reads as preamble, since it sits in no section body, and a divider reads as preamble, since the lines after it open its division as the lines after a heading open its section. Before the first weft the mode is preamble. `probeOf` recognises the line as a heading, arrow, or tilde marker, or plain text, using the same `Probe` regexes the token schemas carry. `isFrontmatterFence` tests one more marker: the `---` that opens or closes the frontmatter block. `isDivider` tests a line of dashes past it. `nextQuote` follows a quote. It opens one on a prose weft whose line opens a fence, which `isProseWeft` identifies, so a fence inside code is left to that code's own language, and it closes the quote on the line `closesFence` accepts. `nextQuote` refuses to open a quote while a box is open, and the section "Marking a box" refuses to open a box while a quote is open, so a box and a quote never nest. `keepsPreamble` tells whether a line within a quote or a box stays in the preamble: it does when the line before it is a preamble weft. `nextFenced` follows fenced code: an opening fence weft sets it from its line, and a closing one clears it. A fence weft that closes fenced code hands the next line to prose, so `modeOf` reads it as prose. `nextAwaiting` keeps the wait for a fence: an arrow with no code on its line starts it, a blank code line keeps it, and any other line ends it.

=>

type Mode = 'preamble' | 'code' | 'prose'

const modeAfter: (weft: LoomWeft) => Mode = byType<LoomWeft>()({
  HeadingWeft: () => 'preamble' as const,
  PreambleWeft: () => 'preamble' as const,
  ArrowWeft: () => 'code' as const,
  CodeWeft: () => 'code' as const,
  FenceWeft: () => 'prose' as const,
  TildeWeft: () => 'prose' as const,
  ProseWeft: () => 'prose' as const,
  FrontmatterWeft: () => 'preamble' as const,
  DividerWeft: () => 'preamble' as const,
})

const modeOf = (prev: Option.Option<LoomWeft>): Mode =>
  Option.match(prev, { onNone: () => 'preamble' as const, onSome: modeAfter })

type Probe =
  | { readonly type: 'heading'; readonly m: RegExpMatchArray }
  | { readonly type: 'arrow'; readonly m: RegExpMatchArray }
  | { readonly type: 'tilde'; readonly m: RegExpMatchArray }
  | { readonly type: 'plain' }

const headingProbe = Option.getOrThrow(getProbe(HeadingStartTokenSchema))
const arrowProbe = Option.getOrThrow(getProbe(ArrowTokenSchema))
const tildeProbe = Option.getOrThrow(getProbe(TildeTokenSchema))
const frontmatterFenceProbe = Option.getOrThrow(getProbe(FrontmatterFenceTokenSchema))

const isFrontmatterFence = (lineText: string): boolean => frontmatterFenceProbe.test(lineText)

const dividerProbe = Option.getOrThrow(getProbe(DividerTokenSchema))

const isDivider = (lineText: string): boolean => dividerProbe.test(lineText)

const keepsPreamble = (prev: Option.Option<LoomWeft>): boolean =>
  Option.exists(prev, (weft) => weft.type === 'PreambleWeft')

const isProseWeft = (weft: LoomWeft): boolean =>
  weft.type === 'PreambleWeft' || weft.type === 'ProseWeft' || weft.type === 'TildeWeft'

const nextQuote = (
  quote: Option.Option<Fence>,
  inBox: boolean,
  weft: LoomWeft,
): Option.Option<Fence> =>
  Option.match(quote, {
    onSome: (fence) => (closesFence(fence)(weft.source) ? Option.none() : quote),
    onNone: () => (!inBox && isProseWeft(weft) ? openingFence(weft.source) : Option.none<Fence>()),
  })

const nextFenced = Match.fn((_fenced: Option.Option<Fence>, weft: LoomWeft) => weft).pipe(
  Match.when({ type: 'FenceWeft', side: 'open' }, (open) => openingFence(open.source)),
  Match.when({ type: 'FenceWeft', side: 'close' }, () => Option.none<Fence>()),
  Match.orElse((_weft, fenced) => fenced),
)

const nextAwaiting = byTypeOr<LoomWeft, [awaiting: boolean]>()(
  {
    ArrowWeft: (arrow) => arrow.source.trim() === arrow.arrow.source,
    CodeWeft: (code, awaiting) => awaiting && code.source.trim() === '',
  },
  () => false,
)

const probeOf = (lineText: string): Probe => {
  const h = headingProbe.exec(lineText)
  if (h) return { type: 'heading', m: h }
  const a = arrowProbe.exec(lineText)
  if (a) return { type: 'arrow', m: a }
  const t = tildeProbe.exec(lineText)
  if (t) return { type: 'tilde', m: t }
  return { type: 'plain' }
}

## Marking a box

A line in prose that starts with a run of colons is a box line, and `boxed` gives its weft the line's `Box` token. Only a preamble weft or a prose weft can be a box line, which `boxedLine` tells, so a run of colons in code stays code, and a run of colons inside a quote stays quoted. `boxLineOf` finds the run through the `BoxFence` probe and records it as the box's fence. It leaves the name and the braces after the run to the tokeniser.

`sideOf` decides what the line does, from the length of the run and whether a box is open. A run of four or more colons is a stray wherever it stands. Outside a box, three colons open one, whatever follows them. Inside a box, three colons alone close it. Three colons followed by a name or braces would open a box inside the open one, so that line is a stray too. `nextBox`, which the chapter "Wefts" defines beside `boxOf`, then keeps the state's flag: an opening line sets it, a closing line clears it, and a stray leaves it as it was.

=>

const boxFenceProbe = Option.getOrThrow(getProbe(BoxFenceTokenSchema))

const boxedLine = byTypeOr<LoomWeft, [inBox: boolean]>()(
  {
    PreambleWeft: (line, inBox) => withBox(line, inBox),
    ProseWeft: (line, inBox) => withBox(line, inBox),
  },
  (weft): LoomWeft => weft,
)

const boxed = (weft: LoomWeft, state: ClassifierState): LoomWeft =>
  Option.isSome(state.quote) ? weft : boxedLine(weft, state.inBox)

const withBox = <Line extends PreambleWeft | ProseWeft>(line: Line, inBox: boolean): Line =>
  Option.match(boxLineOf(line, inBox), {
    onNone: () => line,
    onSome: (box) => ({ ...line, box }),
  })

const boxLineOf = (line: PreambleWeft | ProseWeft, inBox: boolean): Option.Option<BoxToken> =>
  Option.map(Option.fromNullishOr(boxFenceProbe.exec(line.source)), (match) => {
    const content = line.source.replace(/\r?\n$/, '')
    const colons = match[0].trimStart()
    const column = match[0].length - colons.length
    const at = (from: number, to: number): Position =>
      span(
        line.position.start.line,
        line.position.start.offset + from,
        line.position.start.offset + to,
      )
    return nodeOf(BoxTokenSchema, {
      position: at(column, content.length),
      source: content.slice(column),
      health: incompleteHealth,
      side: sideOf(colons.length, inBox, content.slice(match[0].length).trim() === ''),
      fence: nodeOf(BoxFenceTokenSchema, {
        position: at(column, match[0].length),
        source: colons,
        health: okHealth,
      }),
    })
  })

const sideOf = Match.fn((colons: number, inBox: boolean, bare: boolean) => ({
  three: colons === 3,
  inBox,
  bare,
})).pipe(
  Match.when({ three: false }, () => 'stray' as const),
  Match.when({ inBox: false }, () => 'open' as const),
  Match.when({ bare: true }, () => 'close' as const),
  Match.orElse(() => 'stray' as const),
)

## Building the wefts

A constructor emits the partial weft for each kind, carrying `incompleteHealth` for the tokeniser to settle. `makeHeadingWeft` fills the `headingStart` marker, and `makeArrowWeft` and `makeTildeWeft` fill the arrow and tilde markers from the probe match. `makeFenceWeft` fills the run of backticks on a fence line, at the column where it starts, and records which side of fenced code the line stands on. Its label, if it has one, is left for the tokeniser.

=>

const makeHeadingWeft = (
  lineText: string,
  line: number,
  range: LineRange,
  m: RegExpMatchArray,
): HeadingWeft =>
  nodeOf(HeadingWeftSchema, {
    position: linePos(line, range),
    source: lineText,
    health: incompleteHealth,
    headingStart: nodeOf(HeadingStartTokenSchema, {
      position: span(line, range[0], range[0] + m[0].length),
      source: m[0],
      health: okHealth,
    }),
  })

const makeArrowWeft = (
  lineText: string,
  line: number,
  range: LineRange,
  m: RegExpMatchArray,
): ArrowWeft => {
  const start = range[0] + m[0].length - 2
  const end = range[0] + m[0].length
  return nodeOf(ArrowWeftSchema, {
    position: linePos(line, range),
    source: lineText,
    health: incompleteHealth,
    arrow: nodeOf(ArrowTokenSchema, {
      position: span(line, start, end),
      source: m[0].slice(-2),
      health: okHealth,
    }),
    anchors: [],
  })
}

const makeTildeWeft = (
  lineText: string,
  line: number,
  range: LineRange,
  m: RegExpMatchArray,
): TildeWeft => {
  const run = /~+$/.exec(m[0])![0]
  const start = range[0] + m[0].length - run.length
  const end = range[0] + m[0].length
  return nodeOf(TildeWeftSchema, {
    position: linePos(line, range),
    source: lineText,
    health: incompleteHealth,
    tilde: nodeOf(TildeTokenSchema, {
      position: span(line, start, end),
      source: run,
      health: okHealth,
    }),
    anchors: [],
  })
}

const makeFenceWeft = (
  lineText: string,
  line: number,
  range: LineRange,
  side: FenceWeft['side'],
): FenceWeft => {
  const fence = Option.getOrElse(openingFence(lineText), () => ({ length: 0, column: 0 }))
  const start = range[0] + fence.column
  return nodeOf(FenceWeftSchema, {
    position: linePos(line, range),
    source: lineText,
    health: incompleteHealth,
    side,
    fence: nodeOf(CodeFenceTokenSchema, {
      position: span(line, start, start + fence.length),
      source: lineText.slice(fence.column, fence.column + fence.length),
      health: okHealth,
    }),
    anchors: [],
  })
}

~

A plain line bears no marker, so its constructor takes none. `makePreambleWeft`, `makeCodeWeft`, and `makeProseWeft` build the ordinary lines of the three modes.

=>

const makePreambleWeft = (lineText: string, line: number, range: LineRange): PreambleWeft =>
  nodeOf(PreambleWeftSchema, {
    position: linePos(line, range),
    source: lineText,
    health: incompleteHealth,
    warps: [],
    anchors: [],
  })

const makeCodeWeft = (lineText: string, line: number, range: LineRange): CodeWeft =>
  nodeOf(CodeWeftSchema, {
    position: linePos(line, range),
    source: lineText,
    health: incompleteHealth,
    anchors: [],
  })

const makeProseWeft = (lineText: string, line: number, range: LineRange): ProseWeft =>
  nodeOf(ProseWeftSchema, {
    position: linePos(line, range),
    source: lineText,
    health: incompleteHealth,
    anchors: [],
  })

~

A frontmatter line splits two ways. `makeFrontmatterFence` builds a `---`, already whole, and `makeFrontmatterField` a membership or `key: value` line, left for the tokeniser to fill. `makeDividerWeft` builds a divider whole, its dashes and all, since nothing on its line waits for the tokeniser.

=>

const makeFrontmatterFence = (lineText: string, line: number, range: LineRange): FrontmatterWeft =>
  nodeOf(FrontmatterWeftSchema, {
    position: linePos(line, range),
    source: lineText,
    health: okHealth,
    fence: nodeOf(FrontmatterFenceTokenSchema, {
      position: span(line, range[0], range[0] + 3),
      source: '---',
      health: okHealth,
    }),
  })

const makeFrontmatterField = (lineText: string, line: number, range: LineRange): FrontmatterWeft =>
  nodeOf(FrontmatterWeftSchema, {
    position: linePos(line, range),
    source: lineText,
    health: incompleteHealth,
  })

const makeDividerWeft = (lineText: string, line: number, range: LineRange): DividerWeft =>
  nodeOf(DividerWeftSchema, {
    position: linePos(line, range),
    source: lineText,
    health: okHealth,
    dashes: nodeOf(DividerTokenSchema, {
      position: span(line, range[0], range[0] + lineText.trimEnd().length),
      source: lineText.trimEnd(),
      health: okHealth,
    }),
  })

## What this module draws on

Effect supplies `Array.mapAccum` for the fold that carries the state, and `Match` for the choices that weigh more than a tag. `byType` and `byTypeOr`, from the node foundation, build the tables keyed by a weft's tag and by a probe's. The `LineRange` type comes from the line scanner, the marker probes and schemas from `LoomTokens`, the weft schemas and `nextBox` from `Weft`, and `incompleteHealth`, `okHealth`, `Position`, and `nodeOf`, which constructs each weft, from the foundation.

=>

import { Array, Context, Effect, Layer, Match, Option } from 'effect'
import type { LineRange } from './LineRanges'
import {
  incompleteHealth,
  nodeOf,
  okHealth,
  type Position,
  byType,
  byTypeOr,
} from '@athrio/loom-ast/LoomNode'
import {
  ArrowTokenSchema,
  BoxFenceTokenSchema,
  type BoxToken,
  BoxTokenSchema,
  CodeFenceTokenSchema,
  DividerTokenSchema,
  closesFence,
  type Fence,
  FrontmatterFenceTokenSchema,
  HeadingStartTokenSchema,
  openingFence,
  TildeTokenSchema,
  getProbe,
} from '@athrio/loom-ast/LoomTokens'
import {
  type ArrowWeft,
  ArrowWeftSchema,
  type CodeWeft,
  CodeWeftSchema,
  type DividerWeft,
  DividerWeftSchema,
  type FenceWeft,
  FenceWeftSchema,
  type FrontmatterWeft,
  FrontmatterWeftSchema,
  type HeadingWeft,
  HeadingWeftSchema,
  type LoomWeft,
  type PreambleWeft,
  PreambleWeftSchema,
  type ProseWeft,
  ProseWeftSchema,
  type TildeWeft,
  TildeWeftSchema,
  nextBox,
} from '@athrio/loom-ast/Weft'

